---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

export const frontmatter = {
  title: 'Kimi Delta Attention',
  pubDate: '2026-08-09',
  description: 'Attention keeps every token it has ever seen. What if it kept one fixed-size summary instead? Building Kimi Delta Attention from scratch, with interactive figures.',
};
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    To generate one more token, attention re-reads every key and value it has stored for every token
    that came before it. That is why it recalls things precisely — nothing was ever thrown away — and
    it is also why a long context costs gigabytes of GPU memory that have to be dragged across the
    memory bus once per token generated.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    <b>Kimi Linear</b> replaces most of that with a fixed-size summary: instead of keeping a transcript
    that grows with every token, keep one matrix and update it as you read. This post builds that matrix
    up from nothing, watches it break in three specific ways, and fixes each break in turn. The third
    fix is <b>Kimi Delta Attention</b>, and it is a surprisingly small change — one number becomes a
    vector.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
    You need to know what attention does and roughly what a training loop is; everything else gets built
    here. The figures are live, so drag them. And §11 is what happened when I trained all of this myself
    on two RTX 3090s — what reproduced, what didn't, and where I got it wrong.
  </p>

  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-blue-50 text-blue-900">
    <b>TL;DR.</b> Linear attention swaps the ever-growing KV cache for a single fixed-size matrix — a
    lookup table you write into with one token and read out of with another. Three separate things then
    go wrong with it, and they are worth keeping apart: <b>re-writing a key adds instead of replacing</b>
    (the delta rule fixes this, exactly); <b>too many keys crowd each other</b> (nothing fixes this but a
    bigger matrix); and <b>old entries never fade</b> (this is what gating fixes). Gated DeltaNet's gate
    gives each attention head a single forgetting rate. <b>KDA gives every channel its own</b> — that is
    the paper's contribution, and it also turns the decay into a learned positional encoding, which is
    why Kimi Linear's full-attention layers carry no positional encoding at all.
  </div>

  <nav class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 text-sm md:text-base">
    <b class="block mb-1">Contents</b>
    <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-0.5">
      <li><a href="#bill" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The bill comes due</a></li>
      <li><a href="#parentheses" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Why the softmax has to go</a></li>
      <li><a href="#memory" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">That state is a lookup table</a></li>
      <li><a href="#overwriting" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Problem one: writing twice adds</a></li>
      <li><a href="#crowding" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Problem two: too many keys</a></li>
      <li><a href="#forgetting" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Problem three: never forgetting</a></li>
      <li><a href="#kda" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">KDA: one dial per channel</a></li>
      <li><a href="#position" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The decay is a positional encoding</a></li>
      <li><a href="#fast" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Making it fast</a></li>
      <li><a href="#hybrid" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">The hybrid</a></li>
      <li><a href="#results" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Does it hold up?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#wrong" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">Five things I got wrong</a></li>
      </ol>
  </nav>

  <!-- ============================================================ 1 -->
  <section id="bill" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">1. The bill comes due</h2>

    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      Dotted terms open a short aside. Everything in them is detail you can skip — the main line reads straight through.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Softmax attention has a property that is easy to admire and expensive to keep: <b>it never
      compresses anything</b>. To produce token <span class="font-mono text-sm">t+1</span> it needs the
      key and value vectors of every earlier token, in full, unmodified. That is exactly why it is so
      good at precise recall — nothing was thrown away — and it is also the entire problem, because
      those vectors have to be
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">read</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Read from where, exactly?</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The cached keys and values sit in the GPU's <b>HBM</b> — the multi-gigabyte
        pool <span class="m">nvidia-smi</span> reports. The arithmetic happens somewhere far smaller and
        faster: the on-chip SRAM and registers of each streaming multiprocessor, tens of megabytes at
        most.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">So "read" means <b>copied out of HBM, across the memory bus, into on-chip
        memory</b> — once per layer, and again for every token you generate, because there is nowhere
        on-chip to keep a multi-gigabyte cache between steps.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">Why it matters: on an H100 the arithmetic units can perform roughly <b>500
        floating-point operations in the time it takes to fetch one number from HBM</b>. Whichever of the
        two you need more of sets your speed.</span
      ></span></span>
      back for every single token generated.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Attention is charged for twice, in two different currencies. Digesting the prompt costs
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">O(T²) of arithmetic, once</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Prefill.</b> You have <span class="m">T</span> queries, and each attends to
        up to <span class="m">T</span> keys, each comparison a <span class="m">d</span>-dimensional dot
        product. That is <span class="m">T × T × d</span> multiply-adds, so
        <span class="m">O(T²d)</span>.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">It is <b>quadratic, and it is compute</b>. Every query is independent, so it
        parallelises beautifully — you are simply buying a lot of arithmetic. Double the prompt,
        quadruple the work.</span
      ></span></span>;
      generating each new token costs
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">O(T) of memory traffic, every time</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Decoding.</b> Now there is exactly <em>one</em> query. It attends to
        <span class="m">T</span> keys, so the arithmetic is only <span class="m">O(Td)</span> —
        trivial.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">But to do it you must fetch all <span class="m">T</span> keys and all
        <span class="m">T</span> values out of HBM: <span class="m">2Td</span> numbers moved to perform
        about <span class="m">2Td</span> operations. <b>Roughly one operation per number fetched</b>,
        against hardware that wants five hundred. The units idle, waiting on memory.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">So decoding is <b>bandwidth-bound and linear in T per token</b> — and
        generating <span class="m">N</span> tokens drags <span class="m">O(N·T)</span> bytes across the
        bus.</span
      ></span></span>.
      Long-context generation is not slow because the maths is hard. It is slow because the same
      gigabytes get hauled through the same bus, over and over.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      And those gigabytes are not a figure of speech. For a 7B-class model in fp16,
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">a 2048-token context costs exactly 1 GiB</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Count the numbers.</b> Two tensors — one key, one value — per token, per
        layer, per key/value head:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">KV bytes = 2 · L · H<sub>kv</sub> · d<sub>h</sub> · T · p</span
        ><span class="kn-legend"
          ><span class="kn-hd">symbol</span><span class="kn-hd">what it is</span><span class="kn-hd">here</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">2</span><span>one key <em>and</em> one value stored per token — the only constant in the formula</span><span class="kn-val">2</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">L</span><span>layers that keep a cache. In a plain transformer that is every block</span><span class="kn-val">32</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">H<sub>kv</sub></span><span>key/value heads per layer. Equals <span class="m">H</span> unless the model shares them</span><span class="kn-val">32</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">H</span><span>query heads per layer. Does <em>not</em> enter the cache size — only <span class="m">H<sub>kv</sub></span> does</span><span class="kn-val">32</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">d<sub>h</sub></span><span>width of one head, so <span class="m">H · d<sub>h</sub></span> = the model width (4096 here)</span><span class="kn-val">128</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">T</span><span>tokens currently in the context — <b>the one that grows</b></span><span class="kn-val">2,048</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">p</span><span>bytes per stored number, i.e. the precision</span><span class="kn-val">2</span
        ></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Those are not invented: for any model on the Hub they are
        <span class="m">num_hidden_layers</span>, <span class="m">num_key_value_heads</span>,
        <span class="m">num_attention_heads</span> and
        <span class="m">hidden_size / num_attention_heads</span> in its
        <span class="m">config.json</span>. Substituting the 7B shape above, in fp16, at
        <span class="m">T</span> = 2048:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">2 × 32 × 4096 × 2048 × 2<br/>= 1,073,741,824 B = <b>1.000 GiB</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Linear in <span class="m">T</span>, so 32K tokens costs 16 GiB and 128K costs
        64 GiB — for a model whose <em>weights</em> are 13.5 GiB.</span
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>p</b> is the one factor you can change without touching the architecture,
        which is why quantised caches are everywhere:</span
        ><span class="kn-tbl"
          ><span class="kn-hd">Precision</span><span class="kn-hd">p</span><span class="kn-hd">2048 tokens</span
          ><span>fp32</span><span>4</span><span>2.00 GiB</span
          ><span>bf16 / fp16</span><span>2</span><span>1.00 GiB</span
          ><span>fp8</span><span>1</span><span>0.50 GiB</span
          ><span>int4</span><span>0.5</span><span>0.25 GiB</span
        ></span
        ><span class="kn-p" style="margin-top:8px">Note <span class="m">H<sub>kv</sub></span>, not
        <span class="m">H</span>: that is <b>grouped-query attention</b>. Let several query heads share
        one key/value head and the cache shrinks by exactly that ratio — Llama-3-70B uses 64 query heads
        against 8 KV heads, turning 5.00 GiB into 0.62 GiB.</span
      ></span></span>
      of cache. Quantisation and head-sharing attack the constants in that formula, and they help a lot
      in practice — but they leave the <span class="font-mono text-sm">T</span> exactly where it is. That
      is the factor linear attention goes after.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The rest of this post builds a mechanism whose memory is
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">a fixed-size matrix instead — 32 MiB whether the context is 2K or 2M</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p">The same count, for one <span class="m">d<sub>k</sub> × d<sub>v</sub></span>
        state per head per layer. Note what is <em>missing</em>:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq kn-eq-ok">state bytes = L · H · d<sub>k</sub> · d<sub>v</sub> · p&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>← no T</b><br/><br/>32 × 32 × 128 × 128 × 2<br/>= 33,554,432 B = <b>32 MiB</b></span
        ><span class="kn-legend"
          ><span class="kn-sym">L, H, p</span><span>as above — layers, heads per layer, bytes per number</span><span class="kn-val">32, 32, 2</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">d<sub>k</sub></span><span>key width: the <em>rows</em> of the state matrix, one per key channel</span><span class="kn-val">128</span
          ><span class="kn-sym">d<sub>v</sub></span><span>value width: the <em>columns</em>. So each head holds one <span class="m">d<sub>k</sub> × d<sub>v</sub></span> matrix</span><span class="kn-val">128</span
        ></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Setting the two formulas equal gives the crossover: below about <b>64
        tokens</b> the fixed state is the <em>bigger</em> object. Above it, the KV cache runs away and
        never comes back.</span
      ></span></span>.
      The figure below is just those two formulas with a slider on them.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="cache-growth"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 2 -->
  <section id="parentheses" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">2. Why the softmax has to go</h2>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      We want a fixed-size summary instead of a growing cache. Here is what stands in the way. Attention
      for one query, in full:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      O = softmax( Q Kᵀ / √d ) V
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Three matrices. And matrix multiplication is <em>associative</em> — normally you may bracket a
      product any way you like and get the same answer. If we could compute
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">Kᵀ V</span> first, we would never build the
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">T×T</span> object at all; we would build a
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d×d</span> one that does not grow with the sequence. That
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d×d</span> object is exactly the fixed-size summary we are after.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>The softmax is the one thing that forbids it.</b> It works
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">row by row across the whole T×T matrix</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Why that blocks the regrouping.</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Softmax turns a row of scores into weights that sum to 1 by dividing each by
        the total:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">softmax(s)<sub>j</sub> = e<sup>s<sub>j</sub></sup> / Σ<sub>i</sub> e<sup>s<sub>i</sub></sup></span
        ><span class="kn-p">Look at the denominator. To know <em>any single weight</em> you need the sum
        over the entire row — every score that query produced against every key. So the row has to exist
        before you can normalise it, and all <span class="m">T</span> rows make the
        <span class="m">T×T</span> matrix.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">That is what "non-linear" costs you here. A linear function
        <span class="m">f</span> would satisfy <span class="m">f(QKᵀ)V = Q·f(KᵀV)</span>-style
        rearrangement; <span class="m">exp</span> and the division do not commute with the matrix
        product, so the brackets are frozen where they are.</span
      ></span></span>,
      so you cannot know a single attention weight without first computing that query's score against
      every key. The <span class="font-mono text-sm">T×T</span> matrix is not an implementation choice —
      the softmax <em>requires</em> it to exist. Which means the growing cache is not really attention's
      fault. It is the softmax's.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      So the move is to take it out. Not because it is bad at its job — it is very good at its job — but
      because it is the only thing between us and a memory that does not grow:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      O = (Q Kᵀ) V &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;⟶&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; O = Q (Kᵀ V)
      <span class="block mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600 font-sans">with nothing non-linear in between, the brackets are free to move</span>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">But it was doing two jobs. Where did they go?</h3>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is the part I glossed over for far too long, and it matters, because deleting the softmax is
      not free — it was earning its place.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Job one: keeping the output bounded.</b> Softmax weights are non-negative and sum to exactly 1,
      so the output is a weighted <em>average</em> of value vectors — it can never be larger than the
      largest value you put in. Delete it and you have a bare sum that grows as you add terms. That job
      was <b>reassigned</b>, and
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">you can see exactly where in KDA</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Normalisation moved out of the attention and around it.</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The original linear attention (Katharopoulos et al., 2020) kept an explicit
        denominator — it divided by <span class="m">φ(q)ᵀ Σ φ(k)</span> to restore the average. Modern
        variants dropped that and use norms instead. In KDA, from §4 of the paper:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">q, k = <b>L2Norm</b>(Swish(ShortConv(W x)))<br/>o&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; = W<sub>o</sub>( gate ⊙ <b>RMSNorm</b>( attention output ) )</span
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>L2Norm</b> on <span class="m">q</span> and <span class="m">k</span> forces
        every score to be a cosine in <span class="m">[−1, 1]</span>, so no single comparison can be
        huge. <b>RMSNorm</b> on the way out rescales whatever the state produced. And the decay gate that
        arrives in §6 stops the state itself from accumulating without limit.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">Three bounded things in place of one. The paper's Table 6 quietly admits this
        by noting it "omitted the normalization term" to keep the equations readable — the norms are
        there in the real layer.</span
      ></span></span>.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Job two: sharpening.</b> The <span class="font-mono text-sm">exp</span> in softmax is
      <em>exponential</em>: a score of 8 against a score of 6 does not get 8/6 of the weight, it gets
      about 7× the weight. That turns a soft weighted average into something close to a
      <b>hard lookup of the single best-matching key</b> — which is precisely what makes attention so
      good at fishing one exact fact out of a long context.
    </p>

    <div class="my-5 p-4 rounded border-l-4 border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 text-gray-800">
      <b>That job was not reassigned. It was given up.</b> Nothing in linear attention sharpens; every
      stored pair contributes in proportion to its raw key overlap, forever. This is not a detail — it is
      the root of the crowding in §5, the reason a fixed-size memory blurs facts together, and ultimately
      why Kimi Linear keeps one real attention layer in every four rather than going fully linear. When
      you read §11 and see full attention winning on precise recall, this paragraph is why.
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">Now the algebra</h3>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      With the softmax gone, one query's output is just a weighted sum with unnormalised weights:
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">o<sub>t</sub> = Σ<sub>j≤t</sub> (q<sub>t</sub>·k<sub>j</sub>) v<sub>j</sub></span>.
      Three lines rearrange that into a state —
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">the sum moves inside the brackets</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><span class="m">q<sub>t</sub>·k<sub>j</sub></span> is a <em>scalar</em>, so it
        can be slid around freely:</span
        ><span class="kn-eq">o<sub>t</sub> = Σ<sub>j</sub> (q<sub>t</sub>ᵀk<sub>j</sub>) v<sub>j</sub><br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= Σ<sub>j</sub> v<sub>j</sub> (k<sub>j</sub>ᵀq<sub>t</sub>)<br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= ( Σ<sub>j</sub> v<sub>j</sub>k<sub>j</sub>ᵀ ) q<sub>t</sub></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The last step is the whole trick: <span class="m">q<sub>t</sub></span> is the
        same in every term, so it factors out — and the sum over past tokens ends up
        <em>inside</em> the brackets, where it no longer involves the query at all.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">In attention you compare against every key and then combine. Here you combine
        all the keys and values first, once, and compare a single time at the end.</span
      ></span></span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t−1</sub> + k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; o<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t</sub>ᵀ q<sub>t</sub>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The causal mask did not disappear either — it <b>became "the running total so far"</b>. And the KV
      cache is gone: in its place is one matrix per head, the same size at token 10 as at token 10
      million. This is <b>linear attention</b> (Katharopoulos et al., 2020).
    </p>

    <div data-widget="associativity"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Note the crossover in that figure: below roughly <span class="font-mono text-sm">T ≈ 3d</span>,
      quadratic attention is the <em>cheaper</em> option. Linear attention is not a free lunch, it is a
      different asymptote — one you only start collecting on at long context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 3 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ 3 -->
  <section id="memory" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">3. That state is a lookup table</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Before anything goes wrong, let's be concrete about what <span class="font-mono text-sm">S</span>
      <em>is</em>. Take a two-dimensional example and write two pairs into it:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      k₁ = [1, 0]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;v₁ = [5, 0]<br/>
      k₂ = [0, 1]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;v₂ = [0, 7]<br/><br/>
      S = k₁v₁ᵀ + k₂v₂ᵀ = [[5, 0],&nbsp;&nbsp;+&nbsp;&nbsp;[[0, 0],&nbsp;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&nbsp;[[5, 0],<br/>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[0, 0]]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[0, 7]]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[0, 7]]
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Now read it back with <span class="font-mono text-sm">q = k₁</span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-green-200 bg-green-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      Sᵀq = [5, 0] = v₁ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="font-sans text-green-800">exactly what was stored</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      So <b>S is a lookup table built by addition</b>. Writing is "add an outer product"; reading is
      "multiply by a key". That is the whole mechanism, and it is the object every remaining idea in
      this post modifies.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      It worked perfectly there for one reason: <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₁</span> and
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₂</span> are perpendicular. Take that away and things break —
      in <em>two separate ways</em> that are worth keeping apart, because they have different fixes
      and only one of them is the delta rule's.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 4 -->
  <section id="overwriting" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">4. Problem one: writing twice adds instead of replaces</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Write <span class="font-mono text-sm">(k, v₁)</span>, then later write
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">(k, v₂)</span> — the same key, a new value. The state is a sum,
      and a sum has no notion of replacing, so it holds both:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S = k v₁ᵀ + k v₂ᵀ &nbsp;&nbsp;⟹&nbsp;&nbsp; Sᵀk = v₁ + v₂ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="font-sans text-gray-500">(for a unit-length k)</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      You asked for the current value and got the sum of every value that key has ever held. Write it
      eight times and you get all eight added together.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="overwrite"></div>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">The fix: write the error, not the value</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Before writing, ask the memory what it already answers for this key. Then write only the
      <em>difference</em>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> ( v<sub>t</sub> − S<sub>t−1</sub>ᵀk<sub>t</sub> )ᵀ
      <span class="block mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600 font-sans">the delta rule — the parenthesis is the error between what's stored and what you meant to store</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Work the same example: the memory already answers <span class="font-mono text-sm">v₁</span>, you
      want <span class="font-mono text-sm">v₂</span>, so you write
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">v₂ − v₁</span>. Total stored:
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">v₁ + (v₂ − v₁) = v₂</span>. The old value is gone. Toggle the
      figure above and the error drops to zero and <em>stays</em> there however many times you write.
    </p>

    <details class="not-prose my-5 rounded border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-blue-700 hover:bg-gray-100 select-none">Show why this is gradient descent</summary>
      <div class="px-4 pb-4 pt-1 text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-2">
        <p>Treat the state as a little model being fitted during the forward pass. A sensible thing to want is that querying with <span class="font-mono">k<sub>t</sub></span> returns <span class="font-mono">v<sub>t</sub></span>, so score it by squared error:</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs md:text-sm bg-white rounded border p-3">ℒ<sub>t</sub>(S) = ½ ‖ Sᵀk<sub>t</sub> − v<sub>t</sub> ‖²</p>
        <p>Differentiate with respect to <span class="font-mono">S</span>. The gradient of a squared error is (input) × (error)ᵀ:</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs md:text-sm bg-white rounded border p-3">∇<sub>S</sub> ℒ<sub>t</sub> = k<sub>t</sub> ( Sᵀk<sub>t</sub> − v<sub>t</sub> )ᵀ</p>
        <p>Take one gradient step, <span class="font-mono">S ← S − β ∇</span>:</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs md:text-sm bg-white rounded border p-3">S<sub>t</sub> = S<sub>t−1</sub> − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>( S<sub>t−1</sub>ᵀk<sub>t</sub> − v<sub>t</sub> )ᵀ = S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>( v<sub>t</sub> − S<sub>t−1</sub>ᵀk<sub>t</sub> )ᵀ</p>
        <p>Which is the delta rule exactly. So <span class="font-mono">β<sub>t</sub></span> is not a coefficient someone chose for tidiness — it is a <b>learning rate</b>, and the layer is running one step of SGD per token. Expanding the bracket gives the form the paper writes, <span class="font-mono">(I − β<sub>t</sub>k<sub>t</sub>k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ)S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub>k<sub>t</sub>v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ</span>.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div data-widget="beta"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 5 -->
  <section id="crowding" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">5. Problem two: too many keys, and this one has no fix</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Now every key is <em>different</em>, and none are perpendicular. Read one back and you get what
      you stored plus a share of everything else:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      Sᵀk<sub>j</sub> = v<sub>j</sub> &nbsp;+&nbsp; Σ<sub>i≠j</sub> (k<sub>i</sub>·k<sub>j</sub>) v<sub>i</sub><br/>
      <span class="text-gray-500 font-sans text-xs">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;what you wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;everything else, weighted by key overlap</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Concretely, with <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₁ = [1, 0]</span> and a second key tilted to
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">k₂ = [0.8, 0.6]</span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      Sᵀk₁ = [5, 0] + 0.8·[0, 7] = [5, <span class="text-orange-600 font-bold">5.6</span>]<br/>
      <span class="text-gray-500 font-sans text-xs">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;wanted v₁ = [5, 0]; the 5.6 is k₂'s value bleeding through, scaled by the 0.8 overlap</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The leak vanishes only when the keys are mutually perpendicular, and a
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d</span>-dimensional space has only
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d</span> perpendicular directions. Past
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">d</span> keys, no arrangement avoids it.
    </p>

    <div class="my-5 p-4 rounded border-l-4 border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 text-gray-800">
      <b>The delta rule does not fix this one</b>, and I originally wrote that it did. It corrects each
      key in turn, but correcting a later key disturbs an earlier one whenever the two keys overlap — so
      in a single pass it is no better than plain addition, and sometimes slightly worse. What it does
      buy is a <em>ceiling</em>: as you overload the memory, plain error keeps climbing while the delta
      rule's flattens. Crowding is not a defect in the update rule. It is what a fixed-size state costs,
      and only more dimensions buy more room.
    </div>

    <div data-widget="crowding"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      So there are <b>three</b> distinct things that go wrong with a fixed-size memory, and it is worth
      holding them apart — because the rest of this post is three different fixes:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Problem</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">What goes wrong</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">What fixes it</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Overwriting</td><td class="pr-4">re-writing a key adds to it instead of replacing</td><td>the delta rule — completely</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Crowding</td><td class="pr-4">more keys than the state has perpendicular directions</td><td>nothing but a bigger state</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-blue-50"><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Staleness</td><td class="pr-4">facts from long ago still hold capacity</td><td class="font-semibold">the gate — and this is where KDA lives</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="forgetting" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">6. Problem three: never forgetting</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The delta rule stops keys from corrupting each other, but it still never forgets. A fact written
      at token 5 is as vivid at token 500,000 as the day it arrived, competing for the same finite
      capacity. <b>Gated DeltaNet</b> adds a decay
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">α<sub>t</sub> ∈ [0,1]</span> — weight decay on the
      <span class="kn"><button type="button" class="kn-t">fast weights</button><span class="kn-b"
        ><span class="kn-p"><b>Two sets of weights, on two timescales.</b></span
        ><span class="kn-p">The ordinary weights — the projections, the MLPs — are learned by gradient
        descent over the whole training run and then frozen. Call those the <em>slow</em> weights.</span
        ><span class="kn-p">The state <span class="m">S</span> is also a matrix that gets multiplied by
        an input to produce an output, which makes it a weight matrix too — except it is written during
        the forward pass, one update per token, and thrown away at the end of the sequence. Those are
        the <b>fast weights</b> (Schmidhuber, 1992; Schlag et al., 2021).</span
        ><span class="kn-p">It is a useful frame because it makes "weight decay" mean exactly what it
        normally means: shrink the weights a little at every step so old contributions fade.</span
      ></span></span>:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = (I − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ) <b>α<sub>t</sub></b> S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Now the model can choose a memory lifespan. But look closely at what
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">α<sub>t</sub></span> is: <b>one number per head, per step</b>.
      It multiplies the entire state. Everything the head knows fades at the same rate, whether it is
      the subject of the sentence or a stray comma.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 6 -->
  <section id="kda" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">7. KDA: one dial per channel</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Here is the paper's contribution, in full:
    </p>

    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border-2 border-blue-200 bg-blue-50 font-mono text-sm md:text-base text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      S<sub>t</sub> = (I − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ) <b class="text-blue-700">Diag(α<sub>t</sub>)</b> S<sub>t−1</sub> + β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> v<sub>t</sub>ᵀ
      <span class="block mt-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-sans">Kimi Delta Attention, Eq. 1 — α<sub>t</sub> is now a vector, one entry per key channel</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The scalar became a diagonal matrix. Instead of one forgetting rate per head there are 128 — one
      per channel of the state — each computed from the current token. The head can hold a name in
      some channels while letting punctuation wash through others.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="gate-granularity"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      What makes this satisfying is how little of the surrounding machinery it disturbs. Take one
      recurrence, give it two switches — <em>how fine-grained is the decay</em> and <em>does the write
      correct itself</em> — and six named architectures fall out as settings:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-5">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Decay</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Delta rule</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Update</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono text-xs md:text-sm">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">none</td><td class="pr-4">off</td><td class="pr-4">S + k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">Linear Attention</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">scalar</td><td class="pr-4">off</td><td class="pr-4">α S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">Mamba2</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">channel</td><td class="pr-4">off</td><td class="pr-4">Diag(α) S + k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">GLA</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">none</td><td class="pr-4">on</td><td class="pr-4">(I − βkkᵀ) S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">DeltaNet</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">scalar</td><td class="pr-4">on</td><td class="pr-4">(I − βkkᵀ) α S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">Gated DeltaNet</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-blue-50 font-semibold"><td class="py-2 pr-4">channel</td><td class="pr-4">on</td><td class="pr-4">(I − βkkᵀ) Diag(α) S + β k vᵀ</td><td class="font-sans">KDA</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This table is not a mnemonic — in the companion repository it is a
      <a href="https://github.com/eitamar-saraf/kda" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">test</a>.
      One kernel with two flags is asserted, to floating-point tolerance, to reproduce six independent
      transcriptions of the paper's Table 7, and separately to match the Triton kernels the Kimi team
      released. Broadcasting a scalar gate across all channels turns KDA back into Gated DeltaNet
      exactly; setting the decay to 1 turns it into DeltaNet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 7 -->
  <section id="position" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">8. The decay is a positional encoding</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is the most elegant observation in the paper and it is easy to miss. Unroll the recurrence
      and the contribution of token <span class="font-mono text-sm">i</span> to the state at token
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">t</span> carries the factor
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">∏<sub>j=i+1</sub><sup>t</sup> α<sub>j</sub></span> — a product of
      per-channel numbers that depends only on how far apart they are.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Compare RoPE, which multiplies by
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">∏ R<sub>j</sub></span>, a product of per-2-dimension
      <em>rotations</em> whose frequencies are fixed by hand. Both encode distance as a product of
      per-channel factors. RoPE's are periodic and chosen in advance; KDA's are monotone and learned.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="decay-as-position"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is why Kimi Linear applies <b>no positional encoding at all</b> to its full-attention
      layers. Position is delegated entirely to the KDA layers, which handle it dynamically, and the
      global layers are left free to be pure content-based retrieval. It also sidesteps the usual
      long-context headache of stretching RoPE's fixed frequencies past the lengths they were trained on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 8 -->
  <section id="fast" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">9. Making it fast</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Everything above is a sequential loop, which is the worst possible shape for a GPU: thousands of
      tiny dependent matrix updates, none big enough to fill a tensor core. The fix is to process the
      sequence in chunks of <span class="font-mono text-sm">C</span> = 64. Inside a chunk everything
      becomes a few dense matmuls; only chunk-to-chunk state passing stays sequential.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="chunkwise"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Two details in that derivation are worth pulling out, because they are where KDA earns its speed.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Binding both low-rank vectors to k.</b> Multiply out Eq. 1 and KDA's transition matrix is
    </p>
    <div class="my-4 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 font-mono text-sm text-gray-900 overflow-x-auto">
      (I − β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>ᵀ) Diag(α<sub>t</sub>) &nbsp;=&nbsp; Diag(α<sub>t</sub>) − (β<sub>t</sub> k<sub>t</sub>)(α<sub>t</sub> ⊙ k<sub>t</sub>)ᵀ
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      which is an instance of the general <em>diagonal-plus-low-rank</em> form
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">D − a bᵀ</span>. The general version carries two
      <em>independent</em> vectors <span class="font-mono text-sm">a</span> and
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">b</span>; in KDA both are the same key up to scaling —
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">a = β<sub>t</sub>k<sub>t</sub></span>,
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">b = α<sub>t</sub> ⊙ k<sub>t</sub></span> — because that is exactly
      what makes it a <em>delta</em> rule rather than an arbitrary rank-1 update. That tie cuts the
      second-level chunk matmuls from four to two and removes three more: close to 2× kernel
      throughput, obtained for free from a constraint that was already there for other reasons.
      (I have this reduction as a test rather than a claim — writing it caught two mistakes in my
      own DPLR reference before it caught anything about the kernels.)
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>Never form 1/Γ.</b> The chunkwise algebra contains a division by the cumulative decay. Over 64
      tokens with α ≈ 0.8 that reciprocal reaches 10⁶, and in float16 — whose largest finite value is
      65504 — it simply becomes infinity. I measured this: computing that matrix the naive way in fp16
      produced <b>834 non-finite entries out of 4096</b>. The fix is not more precision, it is
      refactoring. Every place the ratio appears it is a <em>pairwise</em> ratio
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">γ<sub>r</sub>/γ<sub>i</sub></span> with
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">i ≤ r</span>, and since the cumulative decay only decreases, that
      is <span class="font-mono text-sm">exp(gc<sub>r</sub> − gc<sub>i</sub>)</span> with a
      <em>non-positive</em> exponent. Every factor lands in (0, 1]. Same numbers, zero overflow: the
      identical computation had a maximum error of 10⁻³, i.e. ordinary fp16 rounding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 9 -->
  <section id="hybrid" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">10. The hybrid</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Here the paper is refreshingly honest: pure linear attention still loses at exact copying and
      precise long-range retrieval, and no amount of gating fixes it, because a fixed-size state
      cannot hold an arbitrarily long verbatim span. So Kimi Linear does not try. It interleaves
      <b>three KDA layers to one full-attention layer</b>, uniformly, throughout the stack.
    </p>

    <div data-widget="hybrid-stack"></div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Three quarters of the layers stop keeping a KV cache, which is where the 75% memory reduction
      and the ~6× decoding throughput at 1M tokens come from. The paper's ablation puts 3:1 at the
      sweet spot: 7:1 trains to a similar loss but generalises worse, 1:1 matches on quality but costs
      more at inference, and 0:1 — pure full attention — is worse than both.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 10 -->
  <section id="results" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">11. Does it hold up?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      I trained the whole family on two RTX 3090s: all six architectures plus a softmax baseline on
      three synthetic tasks, the kernel benchmarks, and a small language-model sweep over the hybrid
      ratio. Some of it reproduces cleanly. Some of it does not, and the part that does not is more
      interesting than the part that does.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">The efficiency claims reproduce, exactly</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      This is the cleanest result of the lot. Timing KDA's chunk kernel against the <em>general</em>
      DPLR kernel computing the same recurrence gives <b>1.8× at every sequence length from 2K to
      64K</b> — flat, which is what you want from a claim that is structural rather than incidental.
      The paper says "nearly 2×". It is nearly 2×.
    </p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/kimi-linear/kernel_bench.png" alt="Left: log-log kernel time vs sequence length, with flash attention crossing above KDA around 4096 tokens. Right: the DPLR-to-KDA speedup ratio, flat at about 1.8x across all lengths." class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. — One head configuration (16 heads, d=128), bf16, RTX 3090. The speedup is verified to be a speedup: the benchmark refuses to report a ratio unless the two kernels first agree numerically.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The left panel also shows the crossover I flagged in §2 as a real thing rather than a caveat.
      At 2048 tokens flash attention is <em>faster</em> than KDA (0.36 ms vs 0.46 ms). By 8K they have
      swapped; by 64K attention is <b>24.6× slower</b> (336 ms vs 13.7 ms). Linear attention is a bet
      on long context, and below about 4K tokens on this hardware it is a losing one.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      At the whole-model level the memory claim lands too. An 8-layer model at 16K context:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Configuration</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Prefill, 16K tokens</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Must be cached</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">64K context</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-2 pr-4">Full attention</td><td class="pr-4 font-mono">2132 ms</td><td class="pr-4 font-mono">512 MB</td><td class="font-mono text-gray-500">out of memory</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-blue-50"><td class="py-2 pr-4">3:1 hybrid</td><td class="pr-4 font-mono">703 ms</td><td class="pr-4 font-mono">129.5 MB</td><td class="font-mono text-gray-500">out of memory</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Pure KDA</td><td class="pr-4 font-mono">219 ms</td><td class="pr-4 font-mono">2 MB</td><td class="font-mono">912 ms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      129.5 MB against 512 MB is a <b>74.7% reduction</b> — the paper's 75%, arrived at from the
      layer counts. And the qualitative difference at 64K is starker than any percentage: on a 24 GB
      card the full-attention and hybrid models simply stop running, while pure KDA keeps going on a
      state that never grows past 2 MB.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">The recall claim reproduces — halfway</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      MQAR turned out to be a much sharper instrument than I expected, and not in a good way. At
      T = 1024 the outcome is <b>strictly bimodal</b>: a run either solves the task (~1.00) or sits at
      chance (~0.04), with almost nothing in between. A three-seed mean is therefore not measuring
      capability, it is counting coin flips. So I ran eight seeds and reported how often each model
      solves the task at all.
    </p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/kimi-linear/solve_rate_mqar.png" alt="Left: bar chart of seeds solved out of 8 — DeltaNet 8, KDA 5, Gated DeltaNet 3, Mamba2 1, softmax attention 8. Right: every individual seed's accuracy, clustered at either 1.0 or near zero." class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. — MQAR at T=1024, 8 seeds, 2 layers, 2 heads. The right panel is the reason the left one exists: there is no meaningful middle to average.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Read down the gating hierarchy and the paper's ordering is right there:
      <b>KDA (channel gate) 5/8 &gt; Gated DeltaNet (scalar gate) 3/8 &gt; Mamba2 (no delta rule) 1/8</b>.
      Finer-grained gating really is more reliable than a scalar gate, and the delta rule really does
      matter more than the gate. That is the paper's contribution, and it holds.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      And then there is the result I did not expect: <b>DeltaNet — no gate at all — solves it 8/8</b>,
      matching full attention and beating every gated model. Palindrome says the same thing more
      gently (DeltaNet 0.954 at T=2048, ahead of Gated DeltaNet's 0.851 and KDA's 0.789), and the
      stack task is saturated for everything (≥0.99) so it discriminates nothing at these lengths.
    </p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/kimi-linear/accuracy_vs_length.png" alt="Three panels — mqar, palindrome, stack — showing accuracy against sequence length for KDA, Gated DeltaNet, Mamba2 and a softmax reference, with individual seeds as dots" class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. — Dots are individual seeds, lines are means. On MQAR the dots sit at the top and bottom of the axis and the mean line passes through territory no run ever occupied — which is exactly why the solve-rate figure above is the honest one.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Why would forgetting <em>hurt</em>? Because none of these tasks ask you to forget anything. Every
      key-value pair in MQAR is queried; every symbol in a palindrome is needed. A forget gate can only
      lose information a model is about to be graded on, so the best gate is one that stays open — and
      an ungated model gets that for free while a gated one has to learn its way there. On that reading,
      KDA beating Gated DeltaNet is precisely the expected result: with 128 independent dials per head
      it can hold some channels open while a single scalar dial has to compromise across all of them.
      The paper's own synthetic figure does not include DeltaNet, so this comparison is not one it makes.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">The headline does not reproduce at this scale</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Kimi Linear's central claim is that a 3:1 hybrid <em>beats</em> full attention. At 34M
      non-embedding parameters and 393M tokens of FineWeb-Edu, it does not:
    </p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/kimi-linear/hybrid_ratio.png" alt="Bar chart of validation perplexity across five configurations: all-attention 51.02, 1:1 hybrid 53.60, 3:1 hybrid 53.82, 7:1 hybrid 57.05, pure KDA 57.81" class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. — 8 layers, d=512, ~34M non-embedding parameters, context 2048, 393M tokens, one seed each. Lower is better.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full max-w-xl text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Configuration</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Layer plan</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Attention layers</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Val perplexity</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono text-xs md:text-sm">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-4">all attention</td><td class="pr-4">AAAAAAAA</td><td class="pr-4">8</td><td class="font-semibold">51.02</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-4">1:1</td><td class="pr-4">KAKAKAKA</td><td class="pr-4">4</td><td>53.60</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-blue-50"><td class="py-1.5 pr-4">3:1</td><td class="pr-4">KKKAKKKA</td><td class="pr-4">2</td><td>53.82</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-4">7:1</td><td class="pr-4">KKKKKKKA</td><td class="pr-4">1</td><td>57.05</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-1.5 pr-4">pure KDA</td><td class="pr-4">KKKKKKKK</td><td class="pr-4">0</td><td>57.81</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Full attention wins at <b>51.0</b>, and perplexity falls monotonically with the number of global
      layers. So the headline does not survive the shrink.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      But look at the middle of that table, because there is a knee in it. Going from one attention
      layer to two buys <b>3.2 perplexity</b> (57.05 → 53.82). Going from two to four buys
      <b>0.2</b> (53.82 → 53.60). Two global layers in eight is very nearly all the benefit that
      global attention has to give here, and doubling them again is almost free of reward while
      doubling the KV cache. That is exactly the trade the paper reports for this ablation — its
      Table 1 notes that 1:1 "maintained a similar validation loss but at the cost of increased
      inference overhead" — and it is the argument for choosing 3:1. That specific claim reproduces
      cleanly at 1/1000th the scale, even though the claim it sits inside does not.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      I want to be careful about what the rest of it does and does not say. It is a
      <b>three-orders-of-magnitude extrapolation gap</b>: the paper's result is 48B parameters and
      1.4T tokens, mine is 34M and 0.4B. A quadratic mechanism that re-reads every token verbatim
      should be expected to win when there are few enough tokens for that to be cheap and few enough
      parameters for compression to buy nothing. What my numbers support is the weaker, still-useful
      claim that <b>a couple of global attention layers recover most of what pure linear attention
      gives up</b> — the 3:1 hybrid closes <b>59%</b> of the distance between pure KDA and full
      attention while keeping a quarter of the cache. What they cannot support is the headline, and I
      do not think a run at this scale could.
    </p>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded border bg-gray-50 text-gray-700 text-sm">
      <b class="block mb-2">Scorecard</b>
      <ul class="space-y-1.5 list-none">
        <li><span class="text-green-700 font-semibold">Reproduced.</span> The ~2× kernel speedup from constraining DPLR (measured 1.8×, flat across lengths). The 75% KV-cache reduction (measured 74.7%). Linear-vs-quadratic scaling, including the crossover below ~4K tokens where attention is genuinely faster.</li>
        <li><span class="text-green-700 font-semibold">Reproduced.</span> The gating hierarchy on associative recall: channel gate &gt; scalar gate &gt; no delta rule (5/8, 3/8, 1/8 seeds).</li>
        <li><span class="text-green-700 font-semibold">Reproduced.</span> The reason 3:1 is the ratio to pick: two global layers in eight capture nearly all the benefit (going 1→2 buys 3.2 perplexity, 2→4 buys 0.2), so 1:1 matches it on quality while paying double the cache.</li>
        <li><span class="text-amber-700 font-semibold">Did not reproduce.</span> The hybrid beating full attention. Expected at 34M parameters; reported anyway.</li>
        <li><span class="text-amber-700 font-semibold">Unexpected.</span> Ungated DeltaNet beats every gated variant on all three synthetic tasks. Consistent with the tasks never requiring anything to be forgotten.</li>
        <li><span class="text-gray-600 font-semibold">Not tested.</span> Everything the paper is actually about: 1M-token context, RL post-training, the 48B checkpoint. Two 3090s do not reach any of it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ 11 -->
  <section id="wrong" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">12. Five things I got wrong</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      Reimplementing this was mostly a lesson in how many ways an experiment can look healthy while
      measuring nothing. All four of these produced plausible curves.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>1. I asserted the wrong mechanism, in public.</b> An earlier version of this post claimed the
      delta rule fixes the crowding in §5 — and the figure beside that claim was, at the time, printing
      numbers that contradicted it (0.51 with the delta rule against 0.45 without). A reader who knows
      decoder architectures told me the post lost him, I went back to check the step he was stuck on,
      and found this instead. The cause was the same as the symptom he reported: I compressed three
      separate failures into one sentence, and the physics did not survive the compression. What the
      delta rule actually fixes is <em>overwriting</em>, exactly and completely; crowding is the price of
      a fixed-size state and only more dimensions buy it down. §4 and §5 are now the corrected version,
      and the figures are two rather than one.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>2. <span class="font-mono text-sm">inf × 0 = NaN</span>.</b> In the decay-weighted Gram matrix
      I computed the exponential first and masked the upper triangle afterwards. Above the diagonal the
      exponent is large and positive, so those entries overflowed to infinity — and infinity times a
      zero mask is not zero, it is NaN. Clamping the exponent before exponentiating fixes it, and the
      clamp is inert on every entry that survives the mask.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>3. A zero bias that erases the memory.</b> The decay is
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">log α = −exp(A) · softplus(·)</span>. Initialising that
      projection's bias to zero gives <span class="font-mono text-sm">softplus(0) = 0.69</span>, and with
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">exp(A)</span> up to 16 that is a decay of
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">e⁻¹¹</span> <em>per token</em>. The state was being wiped between
      writing a fact and reading it. The loss sat at exactly <span class="font-mono text-sm">ln(vocab)</span> —
      not "learning slowly", but pinned at chance forever, because once the signal is destroyed there is
      no gradient left to tell the gate to stop destroying it.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>4. The decay initialisation silently decides the result.</b> Even with Mamba's standard
      <span class="font-mono text-sm">dt ∈ [10⁻³, 10⁻¹]</span>, the same model scored <b>1.00 on MQAR at
      T = 512 and 0.37 at T = 1024</b>. Not capacity — at T = 1024 it still solved four pairs perfectly.
      Purely the initialisation: no channel started with a memory timescale long enough to reach the
      query. Anyone comparing gated architectures without scaling this to their context length is
      partly measuring who got a luckier init.
    </p>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      <b>5. My baseline was crippled and I nearly believed it.</b> Every linear variant carries a short
      convolution, which hands it a "look at the previous token" operation for free. My softmax
      attention baseline did not have one, and it stalled at 0.32 on MQAR while KDA scored 1.00 — a
      result that would have read as "linear attention beats attention at recall", which is the
      opposite of true. Giving attention the same convolution took it straight to 1.00. The full grid:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="w-full max-w-md text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b-2 border-gray-300 text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-6 font-semibold">RoPE</th>
          <th class="py-2 pr-6 font-semibold">Short conv</th>
          <th class="py-2 font-semibold">MQAR accuracy</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody class="font-mono">
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200 bg-green-50"><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">yes</td><td class="pr-6">yes</td><td class="font-semibold">1.00</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">no</td><td class="pr-6">yes</td><td>0.92</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-200"><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">yes</td><td class="pr-6">no</td><td>0.32</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-1.5 pr-6">no</td><td class="pr-6">no</td><td>0.34</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
      The convolution mattered far more than the positional encoding — an induction head has to learn
      "the token after the match", and the convolution simply hands it that primitive.
    </p>
  </section>

